{"title":"Vertex Mobile","sub":"Why is a telecom operator's revenue falling?","type":"Profitability","industry":"Telecoms","geo":"South-East Asia (fictional market)","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"30 min","skills":"Isolating the problem · segmenting · flanker brands","prompt":"Vertex is the second-largest mobile operator in its country. Revenue has fallen 6% in a year, from about $2.12B to $1.99B, while costs are flat. The CEO wants to know what's going on and what to do.","clarify":[{"q":"Revenue streams?","a":"Postpaid (monthly contracts) and prepaid (pay-as-you-go). No big enterprise or fixed-line business."},{"q":"Industry?","a":"Market revenue is flat overall, so Vertex is losing share."},{"q":"Recent changes?","a":"A competitor launched 'Zap', a digital-only prepaid brand: unlimited data for $15/month, sold only via an app."}],"structure":{"root":"Why is revenue down ~$127M?","branches":[{"head":"Which stream?","items":["Postpaid vs prepaid","Subscribers vs revenue per user"]},{"head":"Which customers?","items":["Age, region, usage","Where do they go?"]},{"head":"Why?","items":["Competitor offers","Our pricing & network","Customer service"]}]},"hypothesis":"The new $15 digital brand is the obvious suspect. I'd expect the loss to be in prepaid, among young, data-heavy users.","questions":[{"q":"Here's revenue by stream. Where is the drop?","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["","Last year","This year"],"rows":[["Postpaid revenue","$1.40B","$1.40B"],["Prepaid subscribers","5.0M","4.3M"],["Prepaid revenue per user (per month)","$12.00","$11.50"],["Prepaid revenue","$0.72B","$0.59B"]],"title":"Exhibit 1 · Revenue by stream","note":null,"align":["l","r","r"],"boldLast":false},"answer":"<p>Postpaid is flat. <b>All of the drop is prepaid</b> (−$127M, −18%): subscribers −14% (5.0M → 4.3M) and revenue per user −4%. Losing customers matters about three times more than lower revenue per user.</p>"},{"q":"Which prepaid customers are leaving?","exhibit":{"kind":"c